| # | Severity P1/P2/P3 | Section | Defect | Concrete fix |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | P1 | §2.4, §3.5 — C1 partially open | **The publication bypass is fixed, but the percentage-policy conflict remains.** P1 §3.2 says “没有 `percent` 字段就不显示任何百分比”; `display.ts` deliberately gives categorical cards `percent:null`. Draft 2 nevertheless renders `claim.distribution` as percentages while claiming compliance with P1. Reading stored regime data under the flag is permitted by P1’s server-read contract; that permission alone does **not** authorize displaying it without the eligible F4 card. | Explicitly amend P1 §3.2 to permit clearly labelled, non-probability rule shares **only behind the same-snapshot F4 publication gate**. Keep categorical `display.percent` null. |
| 2 | P1 | §3.4, §8 | **Failed reconciliation still publishes all numbers.** A duplicate contribution, wrong observation, or incorrect total produces `reconcile_failed`, but the spec requires normal numeric rendering plus a warning. That directly contradicts §8’s requirement that displayed contributions reconcile to the card. | Suppress the affected judgment numbers on invariants 1–6 failure. Return a number-free error to the normal UI; retain diagnostic differences in server logs or a separate diagnostic surface. |
| 3 | P1 | §3.3–§3.4 — C7 partially open | **The seven invariants do not validate the rule explanation.** Changing only `rule_id` leaves all seven checks passing: contributions, support, totals and F4 claim remain unchanged, while the UI displays another rule’s label. Stored support is also never checked against that rule’s authoritative coefficients. | Require the rule ID to exist under the row’s series type and stored support to equal its versioned rule support, treating omitted coefficients as zero. Do not reevaluate predicates. Add rule-ID/type/support corruption tests; the enumerated “seven” tests currently omit axis corruption. |
| 4 | P1 | §5.7–§5.8, §10 | **Registering v2 does not switch production to v2.** `judgments/compensate.ts` hard-codes `MACRO_METHOD.version='v1'` and searches the registry for it. Removing v1 makes compensation skip production. `strategy/playbooks.ts` and `strategy/deviations.ts` also accept only v1. The rollout’s “F4 produce … v2” is therefore unsupported. | Include producer selection, version-specific job markers, and downstream method acceptance in the migration. Test actual compensation after a v1→v2 registry change, then v2→v3—not just direct `gather→compute`. |
| 5 | P1 | §5.3, §5.8 — C21 still open | **`official_http` still cannot traverse ingestion.** The ingest route constructs evidence and retrieval records with `adapter:'akshare'`, then calls `ingestEvidence` with that adapter. `ingest.ts` rejects a series whose source adapter differs; its exhaustive `DATASET_OF:Record<Adapter,…>` also lacks `official_http`. Updating `cnSeriesSpecs` and parser dispatch does not fix this. | Derive the trusted adapter from the catalog, partition mixed-adapter retrievals before ingestion, preserve adapter identity on parse failures, and map `official_http` to the intended dataset. Add an end-to-end mixed AKShare/official-HTTP chunk test. |
| 6 | P1 | §4.4, §8 | **A fixed cutoff does not make preset inputs immutable.** `db.seriesHistory` filters `available_at` and current `status='ok'`; unlike `eligibleHistoryAsOf`, it does not enforce batch completion by cutoff. Evidence fetched before the snapshot but ingested/finalized afterward can enter a later request under the **same memo key**. Quarantine resolution can also change membership. Different hashes reveal the difference; they do not prevent it. | Freeze a durable input receipt, or define historical eligibility and an input-identity cache strategy that handles these transitions. Add a batch-finished-after-cutoff test and a quarantine-transition test. Do not claim identical hashes from `asOf` alone. |
| 7 | P2 | §2.4, §3.3, §3.6 | **Coverage abstentions disappear into “no published judgment.”** The specified F4 list URL omits `include_abstained=1`; `cardsOf` removes abstained cards by default. Thus the intended `f4_ineligible` abstention reason cannot be obtained, and insufficient coverage collapses into `f4_none`. | Request abstained cards explicitly, select the current method/snapshot before classifying the outcome, and preserve the actual abstention reason without releasing judgment numbers. |
| 8 | P1 | §3.3, §3.6, §8 | **Null-regime handling has mutually exclusive instructions.** §3.3 returns rows and coverage for non-classified results. §3.6 says an eligible F4 card with null regime is a mismatch, yet its presentation column still requires a decomposition table. D1 and the `f4_mismatch` rule forbid those numbers. The interface also declares `rows:ClockRow[]` while failure rules require `rows:null`. | Define a discriminated response union and one precedence order. Null regime plus eligible claim must be number-free `f4_mismatch`; genuine abstention must remain an explicit number-free state. Remove unreachable numeric states from acceptance fixtures. |
| 9 | P2 | §3.3, §3.5 | **Comparison roles lose valid inputs.** `snapshots.ts` deduplicates observation IDs when `prev` and `prev3m` are the same observation. For quarterly GDP, June is both the previous observation and three months before September. Draft 2 assigns it only `prev3m`, so `display_change` becomes null and the explanation loses the previous-period input. The same happens across a three-month monthly gap. | Allow one observation to carry both roles, or return separate nullable `prev` and `prev3m` references that may share an ID. Test quarterly GDP and a monthly gap. |
| 10 | P2 | §3.2, §3.3 | **The route does not enforce the advertised active-snapshot restriction.** It reads `db.snapshot(id)` and checks versions, but never checks `status==='active'` or the region’s active snapshot ID. A superseded snapshot with unchanged versions can pass the documented sequence. | Explicitly reject or separately classify superseded/failed snapshots. Define the behavior when publication advances between overview loading and the clock request, and test it. |
| 11 | P2 | §4.1 | **The prescribed cutoff field does not exist.** Both `views.ts::Envelope` and the client `OverviewEnvelopeJson` expose `snapshotId` and date-valued `asOf`; neither exposes `env.snapshot.created_at`. The proposed implementation cannot pass that cutoff without another contract change or read. | Specify an explicit snapshot metadata fetch usable with judgments disabled, or add `snapshotCreatedAt` to the overview contract with its dependency/version consequences. Never substitute the date-only `asOf`. |
| 12 | P2 | §4.4 | **The preset route is not fully specified against the actual APIs.** `PRESETS[].form` omits the required UUID `request_id`, so it cannot directly pass `validateAnalysisRequest`. Preset `p6` is `event_window`: POST additionally reads both target-rate histories and derives events, contradicting “each group 2 reads.” The response cards also lack fields required by `RunJson`, including `kind`, `status`, and `created_at`. | Define request-ID handling, event inputs and read limits, and a dedicated presentation DTO or explicit `RunJson` adapter. Budget the two additional event-history reads. The explicit P1 amendment permits shared preset computation without stored runs; this is a bounded preset quota exception, not an arbitrary-analysis bypass. |
| 13 | P1 | §5.2, §5.7 | **Preserving `TRANSMISSION_V1` does not preserve replay by itself.** `buildTransmission` uses caller/default stages and returns current `VERSIONS.transmission`; it does not dispatch from the stored manifest’s method version. “`wiring` selects v2” does not define how old manifests retain v1 configuration and version labels. | Dispatch rendering and replay by the frozen manifest version, with immutable v1/v2 configurations. New manifest construction should explicitly choose v2. Test an existing v1 manifest after the default switches to v2. |
| 14 | P2 | §5.2–§5.5, §5.9 | **The inherited monthly completion witness is omitted from the new series’ availability contract.** `derive.ts::month_end` emits a month only after a later-month observation exists, and includes that observation in `derived_from` and `available_at`. A January LPR quote therefore does not become a January monthly row until a later quote; an MLF operation may wait much longer. “No quote this month,” “awaiting completion witness,” and collection failure are not equivalent. | Document this delay explicitly and distinguish pending completion from absent source data. Add LPR/MLF witness-cutoff tests, including a long interval without another operation. If changing the witness policy, version the derivation rather than silently changing `month_end@1`. |
| 15 | P2 | §5.2, §5.8 | **The six liquidity rows lack a complete, consistent field matrix.** The shared bullet assigns `releaseLagDays:2` and weekly-observation naming to daily, weekly and monthly rows alike; existing tests require daily and `month_end` rows to have undefined lag. `changeKind` is unspecified, although it controls displayed changes and is mandatory in TypeScript/SQL. | Specify each row’s name, frequency, adjustment, change kind, lag and revision policy separately. Restrict weekly scheduling to weekly natives; explicitly enumerate any intended test-policy changes. |
| 16 | P2 | §3.2, §8 | **The clock’s stated read budget cannot build its response.** `db.snapshot` returns IDs, and `regimeOf` returns contributions/results. Neither returns observation values, periods or versions needed by `ClockRow` and manifest-membership validation. The promised “1 snapshot + 1 regime + 2 F4 HTTP” omits the observation read. | Add one batched `observationsByIds` read, define missing-ID failure behavior, and measure the real call count. Test that an unreadable frozen observation causes an integrity/unavailable state rather than becoming `no_data`. |

